@startuml remote-worker-join-handshake
!theme plain
' Source: relux-works/curator-spec PR #134 (CIP-0009 revision 2, commit 5100b46; revision 3 with the owners' decisions of 2026-10-10, commit 5aade0e), cips/diagrams; adapted.
title Remote worker join handshake (v1.3, after Curator CIP-0009 revision 3, 2026-10-10): a server challenge, a signed canonical transcript, atomic invitation consumption; three anchors that must agree: Web PKI, DNSSEC validated inside the client, the invitation's pins; no human second channel

participant "Donor bridge identity\n(curator remote-worker init)" as C
database "DNS (system resolver;\nDNS-over-HTTPS when DNSSEC data is stripped)" as R
participant "Join point\n(our host)" as JP
actor "Operator" as OP
participant "Registry / key keeper" as T

note over C, T
  Anchors before first contact (decided 2026-10-10), all three must agree:
  (a) the Web PKI certificate of the join point's domain;
  (b) DNSSEC validated INSIDE the client from an embedded trust anchor (the donor installs nothing): TXT _swarma.<domain> = root fingerprint, SSHFP = our SSH host key, TXT _swarma-ssh-ca = the host CA, TLSA = the join point's TLS key;
  (c) the invitation's pins when an invitation exists: root fingerprint, SSH host key fingerprint, join point certificate, one-use random code (>= 128 bits), delivered through whatever channel the donor found us by.
  Proof of possession: the transcript signed over a SERVER challenge (not a client nonce).
  No human second channel, no voice comparison. No step downgrades on failure.
end note

C -> JP: POST /join/challenge {invitation_id}
JP -> JP: invitation known, unexpired, not consumed? attempt counter
JP --> C: server_challenge (5 min)
C -> R: TXT _swarma, SSHFP, TXT _swarma-ssh-ca, TLSA (+ RRSIG chain)
R --> C: records and signatures
C -> C: validate DNSSEC locally from the embedded trust anchor;\nsecure: must equal the invitation's pins; bogus or indeterminate: refuse
C -> C: transcript = canonical CBOR, domain-separated:\n{version, invitation_id, project, role, K_sig, K_enc, K_ssh, server_challenge, client_nonce, expires_at, algorithms}
C -> JP: HTTPS validated by the Web PKI, pinned by the DNSSEC-validated TLSA (and the invitation's certificate fingerprint when present)
C -> JP: POST /join/cert {transcript, sig_K_sig(transcript), code}
JP -> JP: code equal (constant time)? signature valid? challenge bound and fresh?\nconsume the invitation atomically -> tombstone(invitation_id, K_sig)\n(same K_sig retry -> same pending enrolment; other key -> refuse); rate limit
JP -> OP: show {invitation_id, fingerprint, name, role}
OP -> OP: check the enrolment against the signed marketplace card when there is one,\notherwise the invitation the code binds; no voice comparison
OP -> T: pin K_sig, K_enc, K_ssh; root-signed admission; grants (scope, expiry)
OP -> OP: package: AEAD to K_enc with AAD = H(transcript); signed by the operator certificate (root-signed, scope onboard)
OP -> JP: release package for <invitation_id>
C -> JP: POST /join/challenge {invitation_id} (second challenge)
C -> JP: POST /join/collect {invitation_id, sig_K_sig(second challenge)}
JP --> C: package
C -> C: decrypt with K_enc; verify operator cert chain to the pinned root;\nverify package signature; check AAD; pin our SSH host key and host CA from the package\nAND cross-check against the invitation and the DNSSEC-validated records

note over C, T
  Pinned afterwards:
  on our side K_sig, K_enc, K_ssh in the registry and K_ssh in the root-owned authorized-keys file of the workplace user;
  on the donor side our root, the operator certificate, our SSH host key, the binding digest, the qualified tuple, the policy generation.
  Every later message: signed over exact bytes by K_sig on the donor, fresh, replay-checked, grant-checked (ADM).
  Replay: cert/collect meet the tombstone; frames meet connection-scoped ids and op_key caches.
  SSH afterwards: host certificates from the host CA (@cert-authority); the worker's K_ssh in a short user-CA certificate (about a day, principal = the workplace user), renewed over the channel.
  Rotation: our host key -> new SSHFP + "init --rotate-host" with a fresh invitation; operator cert -> root-signed record; K -> revoke + new enrolment.
end note
@enduml
